window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"p":"Home","t":"Home","u":"index.html","b":"Showing at FITExpo 2026 AMD 24 The Drone We Built A low-budget drone built from scratch, including the flight controller, by a first-year team that started with no drone experience. Build the drone Read our story Our vision ↓ Exhibition Meet us at FITExpo 2026 We are showing AMD24 and this website at FITExpo 2026. Come and talk to the team about how we built a drone from scratch, what went wrong, and where AMD Labs goes next. Contact us Explore the drone Our simulator, set in a virtual FITExpo 2026 hall. Our vision The intelligent, modular aerial infrastructure for an evolving world. Our mission Providing the adaptable aerial systems industries need to evolve and thrive. Why we did this A low-budget drone at the enterprise level We set out to make a low-budget drone at the enterprise level, and to build it from scratch, including the flight controller. Our story Our story began with our first-year microcontroller project. When we started, none of us knew anything about drones. We had a dream and a proposal, and we had to bring them into the real world. Here you can learn about us and how we built it. We are Team MicroSolutions. After the project, we started an R&D startup called AMD Labs. The full story Our drone on the wooden test rig we built for safe tuning. Your Journey Start wherever you like. Everything we learned is here, including what went wrong. Introduction Who we ar"},{"p":"Our Story","t":"Our Story","u":"story.html","b":"Our story From zero drone knowledge to AMD Labs Our story began with our first-year microcontroller project. When we started, none of us knew anything about drones. We had a dream and a proposal, and we had to bring them into the real world. How it unfolded A first-year project It began with our first-year microcontroller project (IN1901) at the Faculty of Information Technology, University of Moratuwa. None of us had built anything aerospace before. We were Group 39, Team MicroSolutions. Learning from people who had done it We asked seniors, mentors and robotics teams, at our university and others, what actually fails in real drones. Their advice shaped our parts list: an F450 frame, 980 KV motors, 30 A ESCs and a 3S 5200 mAh battery. Working around local supply limits Proper silicone wire and connectors were not sold locally, and importing them was too slow and too costly. So we tested generic connectors by trial and error, and harvested good wire from cheap assemblies. Starting over with a new brain Our first flight controller used an ESP32, but its wireless stack and task scheduler caused timing jitter. We redesigned around an STM32H743 and gave the ESP32 a better job: running telemetry. Taming noise and latency Motor spin-ups locked the I2C bus until capacitors stabilised the power rails. Moving the IMU from I2C to SPI then removed the biggest bottleneck in the control loo"},{"p":"The Drone","t":"The Drone","u":"drone.html","b":"How it works The Blueprint of Our Drone MoonLady Beta 1 is a 450 mm quadcopter with a custom flight controller, its own transmitter and a web ground station. Select a block to explore Flight controller STM32H743VIT6 Sensors MPU6500 IMU BMP280 barometer QMC5883L magnetometer u-blox NEO-M8N GPS Pilot control Custom transmitter nRF24L01+PA+LNA receiver Motors Hobbywing Skywalker 30A ESCs EMAX XA2212 980 KV motors Telemetry ESP32 telemetry bridge Aeronexus dashboard Try it Move the sticks, see the motors These bars use the same motor mixing formula as the firmware. Tilting the model is only an illustration, not a flight simulation. Throttle (µs) 1450 Roll 0 Pitch 0 Yaw 0 Centre the sticks M3 front left, CW M4 front right, CCW M1 rear left, CCW M2 rear right, CW Drag to rotate. 3D model for illustration. M1 rear left (CCW) = throttle + roll - pitch + yaw M2 rear right (CW) = throttle - roll - pitch - yaw M3 front left (CW) = throttle + roll + pitch - yaw M4 front right (CCW) = throttle - roll + pitch + yaw What is inside Airframe and propulsion F450 frame, 450 mm wheelbase EMAX XA2212 980 KV motors Hobbywing Skywalker 30A ESCs 1045 propellers, 2 CW and 2 CCW Power 3S 5200 mAh 60C LiPo MP1584 buck converter to 5 V 3.3 V from onboard regulators Ceramic and electrolytic decoupling Flight controller STM32H743VIT6 MPU6500 and BMP280 on SPI2 Magnetometer on I2C1 Gains stored in internal f"},{"p":"Roadmap","t":"Roadmap","u":"roadmap.html","b":"Roadmap Where we go from here Beta 1 proved the core. These upgrades are how AMD Labs moves it from research prototype toward the enterprise-level platform we set out to build. Precise manual control Replace thumb joysticks with Hall-effect RC gimbals, then re-test sustained hover. This is the top priority, because it is what stopped us holding a hover. Prove autonomous flight Fly and document GPS position hold, return-to-launch and lawnmower survey missions, so that the code in the firmware becomes published results. Chip-level flight controller Move from breakout modules on a base PCB to a multi-layer board with the STM32H7, IMU and passives surface-mounted. Fewer connectors, less weight, less noise. Longer range Replace 2.4 GHz radios with sub-GHz links such as LoRa or ExpressLRS, to go from about 100 m to several kilometres. Better detection Move to fluxgate magnetometers or tuned induction coils, with digital signal processing to reduce false positives, plus proper validation tests. More lift and endurance Heavier sensors need a new propulsion set: lower KV motors, larger propellers and a higher-voltage battery. MoonLady Beta 1. 3D model for illustration. Want to help or work with us? Partners, mentors and fellow builders are welcome. Contact us"},{"p":"Contact","t":"Contact us","u":"contact.html","b":"Contact us Talk to AMD Labs Questions, ideas, corrections, or want to build one yourself? Connect with our team on LinkedIn. Connect with the team MJ Malaka J. Prasad Hardware Interfacing, Sensor Fusion & System Modeling Lead LinkedIn AL Abiram L Communication Protocols, Network Integration & App Dev LinkedIn AJ Achintha Jayawickrama Spatial Data Processing, Telemetry & Hardware PCB Architect LinkedIn NK Nimsara K. Karunarathna Propulsion, Power Systems & Flight Control Loops Lead LinkedIn LN Luxshana N Remote Controller & Wireless Communication Systems Lead LinkedIn The drone and the team. The drone and the team with supervisors. Meet us at FITExpo 2026 Come and talk to the team in person while AMD24 is on show. Code and technical questions Our project is on GitHub . Open an issue to report a problem or ask for help with your build."},{"p":"Build It","t":"Build It","u":"build.html","b":"How to make Build the drone, stage by stage Thirteen stages, in the order that finds faults earliest. Each ends with checks you must pass before moving on. Start at stage 0 Print or save as PDF All stages Your progress 0. Safety, tools and LiPo handling 1. Parts list and sourcing 2. Frame and propulsion assembly 3. Motor and ESC test using a spare ESP32 4. Flight controller board and power rails 5. Flash the firmware 6. Mount the sensors 7. Build and program the transmitter 8. Telemetry ESP32 and dashboard 9. Balance and calibrate 10. Rig tuning 11. First liftoff 12. Survey test Reset progress Read this first This is a record of how a student team built a research prototype, not a certified product or a guarantee of safe flight. You are responsible for your own build and for testing it safely. Parts marked \"to be confirmed by team\" still need detail from us. Stage 00 Safety, tools and LiPo handling A 450 mm drone with 10-inch propellers and a 60C LiPo can cause serious injury and fire. Set these habits before you touch a part. You need Safety glasses, fire-safe LiPo bag, balance charger, LiPo voltage alarm, multimeter, soldering station, thread lock, ST-Link programmer, oscilloscope if you can borrow one Steps Read the whole tutorial once before buying anything. Agree one rule for the team: propellers stay off unless the drone is on a rig or in an open flying area. Charge only "},{"p":"Build It","t":"Stage 0: Safety, tools and LiPo handling","u":"build.html#s0","b":"A 450 mm drone with 10-inch propellers and a 60C LiPo can cause serious injury and fire. Set these habits before you touch a part. Safety glasses, fire-safe LiPo bag, balance charger, LiPo voltage alarm, multimeter, soldering station, thread lock, ST-Link programmer, oscilloscope if you can borrow one Read the whole tutorial once before buying anything. Agree one rule for the team: propellers stay off unless the drone is on a rig or in an open flying area. Charge only with a balance charger, attended, in a fire-safe bag. Never charge or fly a swollen or damaged pack. Keep a separate power switch or a clear way to unplug the battery fast. Check your national drone rules before any outdoor test. Everyone on the team knows the propeller rule LiPo charging setup is ready and fire-safe Tools are on hand, including a multimeter and ST-Link"},{"p":"Build It","t":"Stage 1: Parts list and sourcing","u":"build.html#s1","b":"Parts must suit each other. The motor, propeller, ESC and battery are one system. Our list came from talking to experienced builders. See the full parts list and costs Buy the airframe and propulsion set: F450 frame, 4 × EMAX XA2212 980 KV motors, 4 × Hobbywing Skywalker 30A ESCs, 1045 propellers (2 CW, 2 CCW, plus spares), and a 3S 5200 mAh 60C LiPo. Buy the avionics: STM32H743VIT6 board, MPU6500, BMP280, QMC5883L, NEO-M8N GPS, ESP32-WROOM-32, nRF24L01+PA+LNA. Buy the transmitter set: ESP32 Devkit V1, 2 joysticks, ADS1115, 0.96-inch SSD1306 OLED, a second nRF24L01+PA+LNA, an 18650 cell and its charging and regulator parts. Buy power parts: MP1584 buck converter, a power distribution board, ceramic and electrolytic capacitors, and silicone wire. Test what you can on arrival. Read each sensor's ID register, because identical-looking boards can contain different chips. Propulsion parts are all branded and matching, with no mixed batches Spare propellers are on hand Sensor boards have been checked for clones You have budgeted for breakage"},{"p":"Build It","t":"Stage 2: Frame and propulsion assembly","u":"build.html#s2","b":"Do the mechanical work first, so that faults show up before electronics are involved. F450 frame, motors, ESCs, screwdrivers, thread lock, soldering iron, heat shrink Fasten the arms to the centre plates using thread-locked screws, keeping the frame square. Bolt a motor to the end of each arm. Mount an ESC on the middle of each arm, where propeller airflow will cool it. Solder the three motor leads to the ESC and insulate every joint. You can fix spin direction later by swapping any two. Route the ESC power leads to the power distribution board, keeping them away from where sensors will sit. All screws are thread-locked and arms do not flex Every solder joint is insulated and pull-tested Propellers are not fitted"},{"p":"Build It","t":"Stage 3: Motor and ESC test using a spare ESP32","u":"build.html#s3","b":"Prove the motors and ESCs on their own, before your flight controller and its firmware can be blamed for anything. A spare ESP32, a simple test sketch that outputs 1000 to 2000 µs PWM, multimeter Connect each ESC signal line and a common ground to the ESP32. Calibrate the ESCs so that all four recognise the same minimum and maximum. Follow the ESC manual for the sequence. Drive one motor at a time at a low signal and check the direction. Swap two motor wires if it is wrong. Set the directions to match the firmware: rear left and front right CCW, rear right and front left CW. Run all four at several throttle levels and feel for heat at the ESCs, motors and power board. Optional but useful: measure RPM on each motor with a magnet and Hall sensor at the same PWM. All four should match closely. All four ESCs are calibrated to the same range Motor directions match the table No overheating at any test level All four motors spin at about the same speed at the same signal"},{"p":"Build It","t":"Stage 4: Flight controller board and power rails","u":"build.html#s4","b":"A clean power rail is what stops sensors freezing when the motors spin up. Base PCB or breadboard for early tests, STM32H743VIT6 board, buck converter, capacitors, multimeter. See the schematics and pin tables Set the MP1584 output to 5 V with a multimeter before connecting anything to it. Feed the 5 V rail to the STM32 board and ESP32. Their onboard regulators supply 3.3 V to logic. Solder ceramic and electrolytic capacitors right across VCC and GND at each sensitive sensor and at the STM32 rail. Give the radio a stable 3.3 V supply, because it needs clean power at full transmit strength. Wire the battery voltage divider to the ADC (4:1 in the firmware). Wire the peripherals using the pin tables. Keep power leads away from the signal lines. Power up without sensors first, and measure each rail. The 5 V and 3.3 V rails read correctly under load Capacitors are fitted at sensors and at the STM32 rail No part gets warm when idle You have fixed the battery reading, or have a LiPo alarm and timer in use"},{"p":"Build It","t":"Stage 5: Flash the firmware","u":"build.html#s5","b":"Now the flight controller gets its brain. The firmware boots, tests its sensors and refuses to arm until it has calibrated. PC, STM32CubeIDE v1.13 or newer, ST-Link, USB cable Clone the project repository . Open Aeronexus firmware/ as a project in STM32CubeIDE. The pin setup is in UAV_main_program.ioc . Build, then flash through ST-Link on the SWD pins. Connect USB. The board should appear as a virtual COM port. Use the ST-Link debugger to watch live sensor values if something looks wrong. Firmware builds and flashes without errors The USB COM port appears Sensors detected at boot, with the magnetometer's type identified The drone stays disarmed after boot, with motors silent"},{"p":"Build It","t":"Stage 6: Mount the sensors","u":"build.html#s6","b":"Where a sensor sits decides how noisy it is. These physical steps do more than filtering can. 3M closed-cell foam tape, 3D-printed mast, open-cell foam, twisted wire Mount the flight controller at the balance point of the frame, on layers of foam tape instead of rigid standoffs. This stops motor vibration reaching the IMU. Bolt the 3D-printed mast to the top deck and mount the magnetometer on it, away from motors, ESCs and power wiring. Twist the magnetometer's I2C wires together and route them around the frame's edge, away from high-current leads. Place a small piece of open-cell foam over the barometer's opening, to block propeller wash without sealing it. Mount the radio and telemetry ESP32 on the lower deck. Solder the UART connection between the ESP32 and STM32, crossing TX to RX. Flight controller is on foam at the balance point, facing the correct way Magnetometer is raised on its mast with twisted wiring Barometer has its foam cover Live sensor values stay stable when the frame is tapped lightly"},{"p":"Build It","t":"Stage 7: Build and program the transmitter","u":"build.html#s7","b":"Your hands are part of the control system. This stage builds the link between you and the drone. ESP32 Devkit V1, 2 joysticks, ADS1115, SSD1306 OLED, nRF24L01+PA+LNA, 18650 cell with charging and regulation, Arduino IDE 2 Wire the ADS1115 and OLED to I2C (SDA 21, SCL 22) and the radio to SPI (SCK 18, MISO 19, MOSI 23, CE 4, CSN 5). Power the joysticks from 3.3 V. Connect the joysticks to the ADC: A0 yaw, A1 throttle, A2 roll, A3 pitch. In Arduino IDE, install RF24 , Adafruit_ADS1X15 , Adafruit_SSD1306 and Adafruit_GFX . Open AMD24 mini transmitter/ESP32_Remote/ESP32_Remote.ino and flash it. Release the sticks and read the raw centre values. Update ADC_YAW_MID , ADC_PITCH_MID and ADC_ROLL_MID to match your own joysticks, along with ADC_MAX if needed. Confirm the display reads 0 at centre and that throttle runs from 1000 to 2000. Power the flight controller and check that the signal indicator shows a link. Yaw, pitch and roll read 0 when released, and ±500 at full stick Throttle reads 1000 at the bottom Moving a stick changes the matching axis, in the correct direction Radio link confirmed on the bench"},{"p":"Build It","t":"Stage 8: Telemetry ESP32 and dashboard","u":"build.html#s8","b":"This gives you eyes on the drone: live attitude, PID sliders and magnetometer calibration. ESP32-WROOM-32, PC with Node.js 18 or newer, a 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi network or hotspot In Arduino IDE, install FastLED and open Aeronexus telemetry/ESP32_Telemetry.ino . Flash it. Run the dashboard server, shown below. It serves the site on port 3000 and listens for the drone on TCP port 5000. Change the default fleet admin password and drone PINs in the server code, before using a shared network. With no saved Wi-Fi, the ESP32 starts its own Aeronexus-Setup network. Connect to it, then follow the setup in the dashboard's fleet page to send your Wi-Fi details and PC's IP address. When the ESP32 reconnects, assign the new connection a drone name in the fleet page. Alternatively, connect over USB. The server auto-detects the STM32 as a direct connection. Tilt the frame by hand and watch the attitude display respond. Default passwords and PINs have been changed The dashboard shows live data Tilting the frame moves the attitude display the right way Telemetry returns on its own after a Wi-Fi drop"},{"p":"Build It","t":"Stage 9: Balance and calibrate","u":"build.html#s9","b":"The firmware assumes a balanced drone and accurate sensors. These steps make that true. String, bubble level, dashboard running Balance: with everything installed, including the battery, hang the drone from its centre with string. Move the battery and modules until it hangs level in both directions. Mark the battery position. IMU: place the drone on a level surface and keep it still at boot. The firmware averages 500 samples to find its offsets. Magnetometer: open the dashboard's calibration panel. Slowly rotate the drone through all axes until the points form a sphere. Accept the offsets, which are saved to flash. Keep the battery plugged in and all parts in final position during calibration, and stay away from large metal objects. Check the compass heading against a phone compass. The firmware includes a local declination value, so update it for your location. Drone hangs level on the string Attitude reads about 0° on a level surface Magnetometer points form a centred sphere after calibration Heading matches a reference compass"},{"p":"Build It","t":"Stage 10: Rig tuning","u":"build.html#s10","b":"Starting gains are only estimates. A rig lets you find the limits with no risk of a crash. A sturdy wooden pivot rig, heavy anchor weights, the dashboard, a person dedicated to the disarm control Direction test, propellers off. Arm and give about 30% throttle. Tilt the drone by hand. The motors on the low side must speed up, pushing that side back up. If the high side speeds up, stop and fix it before fitting propellers. Build a rig that holds the drone firmly but allows it to rotate on one axis. Secure the rig so it cannot tip. Fit propellers, checking direction on each motor. Keep everyone clear of the propeller disc. Never hold a drone by hand with propellers on. Tune one axis at a time, with small changes. Start from the firmware defaults for roll and pitch: P 1.2, I 0.5, D 0.012. P first: raise it until fast oscillation starts, then lower it by 10 to 15%. D second: add it gradually until overshoot is damped. Back off if motors get hot or noisy. I last: raise it carefully until the drone holds its angle against a steady push or offset. Repeat for the other axis, then disarm and send the save command. Power-cycle and confirm the gains persisted. The low side speeds up in the direction test, on both axes Rig is secure and can't tip Roll and pitch both recover without oscillating Tuned gains survive a power cycle"},{"p":"Build It","t":"Stage 11: First liftoff","u":"build.html#s11","b":"This is the riskiest step. Keep it short, low and boring. Open flat area, charged battery, timer, LiPo alarm, spotter, pre-flight checklist Complete the whole pre-flight checklist. Test the radio failsafe on the ground with propellers off: switch off the transmitter and confirm the drone disarms. Stand well back, in the open, in daylight with low wind. No one stands in the flight path. Arm using throttle down and yaw right held for 1 second, or from the dashboard. Raise the throttle slowly until the drone lifts off a short distance, then land. Do not chase it with big corrections. Review: if it drifts, oscillates or fights you, return to the rig and to stages 3, 9 and 10. Disarm with throttle down and yaw left. Land by your timer, leaving a margin. Our safe flight time was about 5 to 7 minutes. Go or no-go review done with the whole team Failsafe tested today Liftoff is level, with no strong drift Land within your battery limit"},{"p":"Build It","t":"Stage 12: Survey test","u":"build.html#s12","b":"Use the magnetometer as a sensor, and see what it can and cannot find. Known metal test object, open ground, survey page on the dashboard, GPS fix Start on the ground. Move the drone by hand, powered with propellers off, over a known metal object, and watch the magnetic reading respond. Open survey.html and capture a baseline, using the auto baseline to reduce the effect of outliers. Check the GPS quality badge. The survey rejects points with poor GPS quality or at the wrong speed. Walk or fly a low, slow pattern over the area, with the metal in a position you know. Compare the map with the known position, then export the CSV for your records. Only try lawnmower missions after manual flight is reliable. Keep a pilot ready to take over. Reading changes clearly over the test object Baseline captured away from metal Anomaly appears in the rough area of the object Results recorded in your test log"},{"p":"Learn","t":"Learn","u":"learn.html","b":"How it flies Teach it, learn it Nine lessons taken from a real build, each with goals, an activity and a quiz. Use them to teach a class, or work through them on your own. Start lesson 1 For instructors Print or save as PDF Throttle Controls altitude by motor speed Yaw Rotates drone nose in place Pitch Tilts forward or backward direction Roll Moves sideways by motor speed All lessons Lessons 1. How a quadcopter flies 2. Choosing parts that work together 3. Power and electrical noise 4. Sensors and buses 5. Sensor fusion 6. PID control 7. Real-time firmware and failsafes 8. Radio, telemetry and dashboard 9. A magnetometer as a metal detector For instructors Lesson 01 · 45 min How a quadcopter flies By the end, you can: Explain how four motors produce throttle, roll, pitch and yaw Explain why two motors spin each way A quadcopter has no moving control surfaces. It steers only by changing motor speeds. Our drone uses an X layout, where the two diagonal pairs spin in the same direction. Throttle: all motors speed up or slow down together. Roll and pitch: motors on one side speed up while the other side slows down, tilting the drone. Yaw: each motor's reaction torque twists the frame the opposite way. Speeding up one diagonal pair and slowing the other turns the drone. The F450's symmetry keeps the mixing maths simple, since every motor is the same distance from the centre. Open the"},{"p":"Learn","t":"Lesson 1: How a quadcopter flies","u":"learn.html#l1","b":"A quadcopter has no moving control surfaces. It steers only by changing motor speeds. Our drone uses an X layout, where the two diagonal pairs spin in the same direction. Throttle: all motors speed up or slow down together. Roll and pitch: motors on one side speed up while the other side slows down, tilting the drone. Yaw: each motor's reaction torque twists the frame the opposite way. Speeding up one diagonal pair and slowing the other turns the drone. The F450's symmetry keeps the mixing maths simple, since every motor is the same distance from the centre. Open the motor mixing explorer Using the mixing explorer, predict which motors speed up for right roll, then check. Then draw the X layout and label each motor's direction from memory. Explain how four motors produce throttle, roll, pitch and yaw Explain why two motors spin each way"},{"p":"Learn","t":"Lesson 2: Choosing parts that work together","u":"learn.html#l2","b":"The motor, propeller, ESC and battery form one system. High-KV motors suit speed. Our 980 KV motors with 10-inch propellers suit stable lifting. Here are the sums from our build: Motor peak current ≈ 15 A → 4 motors ≈ 60 A ESC margin = 30 A / 15 A = 2× Battery output = 5.2 Ah × 60C = 312 A available Battery voltage = 3 cells × 3.7 V = 11.1 V nominal, 12.6 V full We rejected a 4S battery because it would over-volt this motor and propeller pairing, and 20 A ESCs because of the risk of overheating. Give students a different motor with a 25 A peak. Have them choose an ESC and work out the battery C rating needed for 4 motors using a 4000 mAh pack. Read KV, propeller and C ratings Check current and battery headroom"},{"p":"Learn","t":"Lesson 3: Power and electrical noise","u":"learn.html#l3","b":"The drone has two very different power needs: huge current pulses for motors and a clean, steady rail for logic. They should be kept apart. Battery to ESCs: thick wires through the power board. Battery to 5 V: a switching buck converter, which wastes far less heat than a linear regulator. 5 V to 3.3 V: small onboard regulators. When motors spin up, the rail dips and rings. On our drone that froze the I2C bus. Capacitors placed right at each sensor supplied local current and absorbed the noise. Read the full story . With an oscilloscope, view a 3.3 V sensor rail while a noisy load switches. Add a ceramic and an electrolytic capacitor at the sensor and compare. No propellers are needed. Explain why switching regulators beat linear ones here Explain what decoupling capacitors do"},{"p":"Learn","t":"Lesson 4: Sensors and buses","u":"learn.html#l4","b":"Bus Wires Typical speed here On our drone SPI 4, with one select per chip Up to 20 MHz on the MPU6500 IMU, barometer, radio I2C 2, shared by address Up to 400 kHz Magnetometer UART 2, point to point 115200 baud GPS, ESP32 Reading the IMU over I2C limited our control loop, so we moved it to SPI. The magnetometer stayed on I2C because it updates more slowly. But I2C can lock up, so the firmware has a recovery routine: it toggles the clock 9 times to release the bus. Work out how long a 14-byte IMU read takes at 400 kHz I2C and at 10 MHz SPI, ignoring overhead. Then compare both with the 4000 µs loop period. Compare I2C, SPI and UART Say what each drone sensor measures"},{"p":"Learn","t":"Lesson 5: Sensor fusion","u":"learn.html#l5","b":"A gyroscope is smooth but drifts over time. An accelerometer knows where gravity points but is badly affected by vibration. Sensor fusion uses each to correct the other. Filter: a hardware filter of about 41 Hz, then a software filter. Correct: remove false acceleration when the IMU sits away from the centre of rotation. Compare: the Mahony filter compares gravity as predicted from the gyro with gravity as measured, using a cross product. Adjust: a small PI loop nudges the gyro to remove the error and learn its drift. Yaw: gravity cannot sense heading, so yaw blends 98% gyro with 2% magnetometer. Orientation is stored as a quaternion, which avoids the gimbal lock of Euler angles. Log roll angle from the dashboard while tilting the board by hand. Then tap the frame and watch how little the filtered value reacts. Explain why neither gyro nor accelerometer is enough Describe what the Mahony filter does"},{"p":"Learn","t":"Lesson 6: PID control","u":"learn.html#l6","b":"PID turns an error into a correction. The firmware runs it 250 times a second. P pushes in proportion to the error now. Too much makes fast oscillation. I adds up small errors over time. It removes steady offsets, but too much makes slow rocking. D resists fast change, like a shock absorber. Too much amplifies noise and heats motors. P I D Add a steady push, like wind or an off-centre CG Starts at 15° tilt and tries to reach level. Simplified teaching model. Do not copy these gains to a real drone. Our firmware adds refinements to the textbook version: D on the measurement, to prevent a kick when sticks move suddenly. A low-pass filter on the D term. Feedforward for a sharper stick response. Pausing the I term when outputs are saturated, to prevent windup. Shortest-path yaw error, so 350° to 10° is a 20° turn. TPA, which reduces P and D at high throttle. On the simulator, set I and D to 0 and raise P until it oscillates. Add D until it settles. Turn on the steady push, see the offset, then add I. Later, repeat on a rig with a real axis. Describe what P, I and D each do Tune a simple system in the right order"},{"p":"Learn","t":"Lesson 7: Real-time firmware and failsafes","u":"learn.html#l7","b":"A flight controller must run on time, every time. Our main loop is driven by a hardware timer: TIM4 interrupt every 4000 µs → sets pid_loop_flag while (1): if pid_loop_flag: read IMU → fusion → PID → motor mix → set PWM (250 Hz) every 5th loop: barometer + magnetometer (50 Hz) every 50th loop: send telemetry JSON (5 Hz) Nothing here should block. UARTs use interrupts with lock flags, the radio is polled once per loop, and hardware timers generate PWM in the background. Gains are stored in internal flash, with a magic number that detects blank memory on first boot. Failsafes cover radio loss, dashboard loss, geofence breach, crashes and I2C faults. They need testing, and they can be wrong: our low-battery failsafe is currently inactive because the battery reading is hardcoded. See the status list . With propellers off and the drone armed, switch off the transmitter and record how long it takes to react. It should be about 500 ms. Discuss why a failsafe needs testing. As an extension, propose a fix for the battery reading. Explain why timing must be deterministic Describe the main failsafes"},{"p":"Learn","t":"Lesson 8: Radio, telemetry and dashboard","u":"learn.html#l8","b":"The drone has two separate links: Control: Joysticks → ADS1115 → ESP32 → nRF24 ))) nRF24 → STM32 50 Hz, 8 bytes Telemetry: STM32 → UART → ESP32 ))) Wi-Fi → Node.js → Socket.IO → browser 5 Hz, JSON The control packet has four 16-bit values, packed with #pragma pack so both chips agree on its exact layout. A low 250 kbps data rate gives better receiver sensitivity and more range, and 8 bytes needs very little bandwidth. The dashboard limits its heavy chart updates to 1 Hz, so the page stays responsive. Pick one value, such as roll angle. In the source code, follow it from main.c to ESP32_Telemetry.ino , server.js and script.js . Draw the path. Trace data from sensor to browser Explain why data rate affects range"},{"p":"Learn","t":"Lesson 9: A magnetometer as a metal detector","u":"learn.html#l9","b":"Earth's magnetic field is fairly uniform locally. Ferrous objects distort it. Fly a magnetometer over an area and tag each reading with GPS, and those distortions appear on a map. The dashboard's survey view cleans the data. It rejects poor GPS and readings taken while turning, corrects for altitude and slow drift, and draws heatmaps. Limits: the sensor is a consumer compass chip and GPS error is 2 to 5 m. It can find a zone, but not an exact point, depth or shape. A signal can also come from the drone itself, which is why mounting and calibration matter. Survey heatmap. 3D surface. Lay out a 1 m grid on the floor. Move a magnetometer board by hand over a hidden steel object and record the reading at each point. Plot it and locate the object. Repeat at twice the height. Explain how ferrous metal shows up in magnetic data State the method's limits honestly"},{"p":"Learn","t":"For instructors: schedule and rubric","u":"learn.html#teach","b":"course schedule rubric marking teaching guide instructor"},{"p":"Challenges","t":"Starting with no drone experience","u":"challenges.html#c1","b":"It was our first aerospace project, and all five of us were new to it. Wrong motor, ESC or propeller choices could have doomed the build before any code was written. The gap was knowledge of thrust, current draw and which parts work together. We asked seniors, mentors and robotics teams at our university and others about real failures. Their advice shaped a parts list where the frame, motors, ESCs and battery suit each other. Ask people who have failed already. It is the cheapest test there is."},{"p":"Challenges","t":"Parts we could not buy locally","u":"challenges.html#c2","b":"Suitable wire and connectors were not sold in the local market. High-current silicone wire and vibration-proof connectors were unavailable, and importing cost too much and took too long. Regional supply limits against a fixed academic deadline. We tested locally available connectors for current capacity, grip under vibration and ease of soldering. We kept the safest connectors and harvested quality wire from cheap assemblies for the power and sensor harnesses. Check real capability rather than the label, and look at what is already inside cheap products."},{"p":"Challenges","t":"An ESP32 cannot be a flight controller","u":"challenges.html#c3","b":"Our first brain could not keep steady timing. Control timing was unstable during early firmware prototypes. Wi-Fi and Bluetooth stacks and the FreeRTOS scheduler added unpredictable jitter and latency. We reviewed how established systems such as Pixhawk and Betaflight are built, and found they rely on STM32 chips. We redesigned around the STM32H743, with its hardware floating point, and repurposed the ESP32 for telemetry. Pick a chip for its timing guarantees as well as its clock speed."},{"p":"Challenges","t":"I2C bus freezing as motors spin up","u":"challenges.html#c4","b":"Sensor data stopped whenever the ESCs drew hard. The I2C bus to the sensors randomly froze during motor spin-ups, which would crash a drone in flight. An oscilloscope showed voltage dips and high-frequency noise on shared power rails during high-current draw. We probed the rails and bus lines with an oscilloscope while spinning the motors. We soldered ceramic and electrolytic capacitors across VCC and GND at the sensors and STM32 rail, and later added a software bus recovery. Fix noise at its source first. Software recovery is a backup."},{"p":"Challenges","t":"Clone chips under the same name","u":"challenges.html#c5","b":"The same part name could hide different silicon. Sensor boards sold under one name contained different chips, with different addresses and registers. Regional vendors and batch variation, for example HMC5883L, QMC5883L and Si24R1 clones. Fixed drivers crashed or read garbage on a chip that did not match the datasheet. At boot, the firmware scans the bus, identifies the chip and loads the correct driver and scaling. Assume parts will vary, and detect them at boot."},{"p":"Challenges","t":"No budget for a test bench","u":"challenges.html#c6","b":"Free-flight tuning risked destroying the drone. Starting PID values are only estimates, so a free-flight mistake could end the project. Professional multi-axis test benches were far beyond our budget. We looked at what such benches actually do: restrict movement while allowing rotation. We built wooden rigs that lock the drone in place and let it rotate on one axis, so gains could be pushed safely to their limits. You often need the principle behind expensive equipment, rather than the equipment itself."},{"p":"Challenges","t":"Uneven thrust from budget motors","u":"challenges.html#c7","b":"The drone drifted, and it was not the software. After static tuning, the drone drifted in pitch and roll on every liftoff attempt. Cheap unbranded motors and ESCs spun at different speeds from the same signal. Debugging confirmed correct PWM output. Magnets and Hall sensors on the motors then measured each motor's RPM. We replaced them with branded motors and ESCs, which gave matching speeds and a level liftoff. Measure the physical output before spending more time on code."},{"p":"Challenges","t":"I2C slowing the control loop","u":"challenges.html#c8","b":"The fast chip spent its time waiting. Timing analysis showed the loop was limited even with a fast processor. Reading the IMU over I2C, typically limited to 400 kHz, left the CPU waiting on the bus. We measured control loop timing and traced where the time was going. We moved the MPU6500 to SPI and refactored the driver, which removed the bottleneck. The slowest link sets your speed, however fast the processor is."},{"p":"Challenges","t":"Holding a hover by hand","u":"challenges.html#c9","b":"Stable on liftoff, but not controllable in the air. After stable vertical liftoff, the pilot could not hold a sustained hover. Thumb joysticks lack fine travel and spring precision, while the drone responds very quickly. Post-flight review pointed to over-correction by the pilot, known as pilot-induced oscillation. Still open. Our next step is Hall-effect RC gimbals, followed by a repeat hover test. Your control input is part of the control system too."},{"p":"Challenges","t":"Radio range of about 100 m","u":"challenges.html#c10","b":"The radio set the working area. Beyond about 100 m in line of sight, signal strength dropped quickly and packets were lost. The physical limits of 2.4 GHz nRF24L01+ modules, plus local interference. Field tests of the link at increasing distance. Still open. We lowered the data rate to 250 kbps to get the best range. Sub-GHz LoRa or ExpressLRS is the planned upgrade. Design missions around the range you have measured, not the range on the box."},{"p":"Challenges","t":"Rough metal location","u":"challenges.html#c11","b":"It can find a zone, not a point. Survey maps show where a disturbance is roughly, but not its exact position, depth or shape. A consumer magnetometer plus GPS with about 2 to 5 m of error. We analysed the collected survey data against what we were trying to locate. Accepted for now. We class it as a preliminary sweeping platform that guides follow-up work on the ground. Say clearly what your sensor can tell you, and what it cannot."},{"p":"Challenges","t":"Only 5 to 7 minutes of flight","u":"challenges.html#c12","b":"High current draw limits survey time. Safe flight time is about 5 to 7 minutes, too short for wide-area surveys. The current drawn by the motors and electronics from one 3S 5200 mAh battery. Flight endurance analysis of the prototype. Still open. Battery swaps for now. A propulsion redesign is on the roadmap. Do the flight time sums early, and include everything on board."},{"p":"Challenges","t":"Little spare lift","u":"challenges.html#c13","b":"Adding parts used up the margin. The custom PCB, modules and printed enclosures pushed the platform close to its lifting limit. The finite thrust of an F450 with 10-inch propellers. Reviewing the weight added during integration. Still open. Heavier sensors will need lower KV motors, larger propellers and a higher-voltage battery. Keep a weight budget from day one. Every gram adds up."},{"p":"Challenges","t":"Challenges","u":"challenges.html","b":"Challenges What went wrong and what we did Eight problems we solved along the way, and five limits we are still working on. The problems taught us more than the wins. All 13 People and supply Hardware Firmware Flight and limits 01 Starting with no drone experience It was our first aerospace project, and all five of us were new to it. › Symptom Wrong motor, ESC or propeller choices could have doomed the build before any code was written. Cause The gap was knowledge of thrust, current draw and which parts work together. How we found it We asked seniors, mentors and robotics teams at our university and others about real failures. Fix Their advice shaped a parts list where the frame, motors, ESCs and battery suit each other. Lesson Ask people who have failed already. It is the cheapest test there is. Go deeper Related section 02 Parts we could not buy locally Suitable wire and connectors were not sold in the local market. › Symptom High-current silicone wire and vibration-proof connectors were unavailable, and importing cost too much and took too long. Cause Regional supply limits against a fixed academic deadline. How we found it We tested locally available connectors for current capacity, grip under vibration and ease of soldering. Fix We kept the safest connectors and harvested quality wire from cheap assemblies for the power and sensor harnesses. Lesson Check real capability ra"},{"p":"Resources","t":"Resources","u":"resources.html","b":"Resources Everything in one place Source code, wiring, costs, checklists and a glossary. Pages print cleanly, so you can save any of them as a PDF. Print or save as PDF On this page Resources Source code Wiring and pins Schematics Build notes Cost breakdown Pre-flight checklist Troubleshooting Test log Drone rules Glossary Credits Source code The firmware, transmitter, telemetry and dashboard code are all on GitHub under the MIT License. Vendor libraries in the repository keep their own licences. Folder Contents Aeronexus firmware/ STM32 flight controller AMD24 mini transmitter/ ESP32 transmitter Aeronexus telemetry/ ESP32 Wi-Fi bridge Aeronexus dashboard/ Node.js ground station pid_tuning_guide.md PID tuning reference Open repository Wiring and pins Flight controller (STM32H743) Peripheral Pins Connected to Settings SPI1 PA5 SCK, PA6 MISO, PA7 MOSI, PA4 CSN, PC4 CE nRF24L01+ 250 kbps, channel 76 SPI2 PB10 SCK, PC2 MISO, PC1 MOSI, PB12 CS IMU, PB11 CS baro MPU6500, BMP280 500°/s, ±8 g I2C1 PB6 SCL, PB7 SDA Magnetometer Auto-detected USART2 PA2, PA3 ESP32 telemetry 115200 baud USART3 PD9 TX, PD2 RX GPS 115200 baud TIM3 PC6, PC7, PC8, PB1 ESCs M1 to M4 1100 to 2000 µs TIM4 Internal Control loop timer 250 Hz GPIO PE3, PE4 Arm/disarm and spare buttons 500 ms debounce GPIO PA1 Status LED Solid when armed, fast blink on failsafe USB FS USB Virtual COM port Telemetry and commands Moto"},{"p":"Resources","t":"Wiring and pins","u":"resources.html#wiring","b":"SPI1 PA5 SCK, PA6 MISO, PA7 MOSI, PA4 CSN, PC4 CE nRF24L01+ 250 kbps, channel 76 SPI2 PB10 SCK, PC2 MISO, PC1 MOSI, PB12 CS IMU, PB11 CS baro MPU6500, BMP280 500°/s, ±8 g I2C1 PB6 SCL, PB7 SDA Magnetometer Auto-detected USART2 PA2, PA3 ESP32 telemetry 115200 baud USART3 PD9 TX, PD2 RX GPS 115200 baud TIM3 PC6, PC7, PC8, PB1 ESCs M1 to M4 1100 to 2000 µs TIM4 Internal Control loop timer 250 Hz GPIO PE3, PE4 Arm/disarm and spare buttons 500 ms debounce GPIO PA1 Status LED Solid when armed, fast blink on failsafe USB FS USB Virtual COM port Telemetry and commands ADS1115 SDA 21, SCL 22 A0 yaw, A1 throttle, A2 roll, A3 pitch OLED SSD1306 SDA 21, SCL 22 Address 0x3C nRF24L01+ SCK 18, MISO 19, MOSI 23, CE 4, CSN 5 Address 00001, channel 76, 250 kbps, 8-byte payload"},{"p":"Resources","t":"Cost breakdown","u":"resources.html#cost","b":"price budget rupees parts list bom"},{"p":"Resources","t":"Pre-flight checklist","u":"resources.html#preflight","b":"Battery fully charged, undamaged, not swollen Flight timer ready, LiPo alarm fitted and audible Propellers tight, undamaged, correct direction on each motor Arms, motors and landing gear firm, no loose screws Battery strapped down and CG checked Wires clear of propellers Transmitter charged, throttle at zero Radio link and dashboard connected IMU calibrated with the drone still and level Compass heading correct, GPS fix if required Open area, clear weather, daylight, low wind People kept at a safe distance, spotter briefed Failsafe tested today with propellers off Local drone rules checked"},{"p":"Resources","t":"Troubleshooting","u":"resources.html#trouble","b":"Drone flips on takeoff Motor order wrong, or propellers on the wrong motors Recheck stage 3 and stage 10's direction test, props off Motors speed up on the wrong side Motor order or mixing wrong Stop. Do not fit propellers until fixed Fast buzzing, hot motors P or D too high Lower P, or lower D if the motors run hot Slow rocking at 1 to 2 Hz I too high Lower I Bouncing after a correction D too low Raise D Drifts with sticks centred I too low, CG off or unequal motors Check CG, compare motor RPM, then raise I Oscillation only at high throttle TPA not lowering gains enough Adjust TPA in firmware Altitude bounces in altitude hold Altitude P too high or D too low Lower P or raise D, and check the barometer's foam cover I2C freezes during spin-up Power rail noise Add capacitors, separate wiring and check the buck converter Compass heading wanders Motor or power cable interference Raise the mast, twist wires and recalibrate Gains lost after power cycle Not saved to flash Send the save command while disarmed Telemetry not reaching dashboard Wrong Wi-Fi settings or server IP Rerun the Aeronexus-Setup step and check your firewall allows TCP port 5000 Dashboard always shows 13.2 V The firmware's battery value is hardcoded Expected in current code. See the battery warning in stage 4"},{"p":"Resources","t":"Glossary","u":"resources.html#gloss","b":"AHRS Attitude and Heading Reference System: works out the drone's orientation from its sensors. AirMode Keeps motors above idle so the PID loop can still steer at low throttle. BLDC Brushless DC motor. Buck converter An efficient circuit that steps voltage down. CG Centre of gravity. CW / CCW Clockwise and counter-clockwise. Decoupling capacitor A small energy store near a chip that smooths its power supply. ESC Electronic Speed Controller: drives a brushless motor from a control signal. Feedforward A PID add-on that reacts directly to stick movement. Geofence A distance limit that triggers a safety response. Hard-iron distortion A constant magnetic offset caused by the drone's own parts. Hall-effect gimbal A joystick that senses position with magnets, with no sliding contacts. HDOP A GPS quality number. Lower is better. IMU Inertial Measurement Unit: a gyroscope plus an accelerometer. KV Motor speed in RPM per volt, with no load. LiPo Lithium polymer battery. Powerful, and needs careful handling. Mahony filter The sensor fusion method used to combine gyro and accelerometer data. PID Proportional, Integral, Derivative: the core feedback control method. PIO Pilot-induced oscillation: over-corrections by the pilot that make motion worse. PWM Pulse Width Modulation: here, a 1000 to 2000 µs pulse that sets ESC speed. Quaternion A four-number way to represent rotation without gimbal"},{"p":"Resources","t":"Schematics","u":"resources.html#schem","b":"circuit diagram flight controller transmitter"}];
